f00a6bdcff 2026-10-29 Name each chat to JusPrin's gateway, so it can keep the chat whole
The gateway now keeps each chat's newest request and reply for future
fine-tuning (tsd-enterprise 906752ee3e). Without a chat name it can only
keep each turn separately.

Every reply request to the gateway now carries X-JusPrin-Chat: the first 32
hex digits of SHA-256 over the chat's key, "<projectId>/<conversationId>".
It is the same on every request of a chat and differs between chats. The
project id is random, and a project copy imported on the same machine
already gets a new one (fork_identity), so a copy's chats get new names
without storing anything new in the project. Titles carry no chat key and
so no header. Other providers get nothing.

The key travels as AgentRequest::chat_key, set by
AgentHost::make_agent_request, and TurnTranscript::chat_key. Those lines
are a08d17471f's on claude/chat-history-cache byte for byte, without its
use as OpenAI's prompt_cache_key, so merging that branch later applies
cleanly.

Entire-Checkpoint: 01M64TZN1F72DEA1CD3K10AZHC

---
          </Row>
        ) : (
          <Row title="Model" detail="Add a key above, and new chats start with that provider’s model." />
        )}
      </Group>
      <div className="prefs-callout">
        <span className="jp-icon jp-icon-lock" aria-hidden="true" />
        <p>
          The provider a chat uses receives your messages, the open project’s settings and anything you attach. With a
          JusPrin account, JusPrin receives them and passes them on to the model provider it uses. JusPrin doesn’t send
          your keys anywhere else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  );
}
---
    // The credential is a JusPrin account's sign-in rather than an API key,
    // and the endpoint is JusPrin's gateway. Every request names its turn
    // (X-JusPrin-Turn) and says whether it is a title (X-JusPrin-Request),
    // because the gateway charges the account once per turn and never for a
    // title; a refusal means the sign-in has ended, and 402 means the
    // account's allowance is used up. A reply also names its chat
    // (X-JusPrin-Chat), so the gateway can keep the chat whole.
    bool           account_sign_in{false};
    // The provider's own web search, as the Responses API tools list names
    // it: OpenAI's spelling, which JusPrin's gateway passes to OpenAI, unless
    // the row gives its own. The Messages API has one spelling, which
    // AnthropicCodec writes.
    std::string    web_search_type{"web_search"};
};

